=== ANSWER A ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: git ls-files | wc -l && git log -3 --format='%h %an <%ae> %s' && ls -a; cat .git-blame-ignore-revs 2>/dev/null
 - Bash: cat > /tmp/blame.sh <<'EOF'
mode="$1"
git ls-files | while read f; do
  case "$f" in *.*) ext="${f##*.}";; *) ext="(none)";; esac
  git blame --line-porcelain $mode -- "$f" | awk -v e="$ext" '/^author /{a=substr($0,8)} /^author-mail /{print e "\t" a " " substr($0,13)}'
done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr
EOF
echo RAW; sh /tmp/blame.sh ""; echo; echo IGNORE-REFORMAT; sh /tmp/blame.sh "--ignore-rev 62628c0"; git shortlog -sne --all
FINAL ANSWER:
For each file type, the person who owns the most surviving lines (from `git blame` across all 18 tracked files):

| Type | Owner by raw blame | Owner if the reformat commit is skipped |
|---|---|---|
| `.js` (lib + tests) | **Fiona Formatter**: 1016 of ~1203 lines | **Troy Goode**: 520 (473 + 47 under two emails), then Douglas Christopher Wilson 339, then Fiona 237 |
| `.md` (README, HISTORY) | **Troy Goode**: 104 | same |
| `.yml` (.eslintrc, workflows) | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 117 | same |
| `.json` (package.json) | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 20 | same |
| `.gitignore` | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 4 of 5 | same |
| no extension (LICENSE) | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 17 of 22 | same |

There are two caveats, both affecting the `.js` row only:

- **The reformat commit skews the result.** The latest commit, `62628c0` ("reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes"), puts Fiona Formatter on most JS lines even though nothing was really authored there. Telling `git blame` to skip that commit gives JS back to Troy Goode. Fiona still keeps 237 lines, because blame can't match lines where the quote style changed. So Troy's real share is likely higher than 520. If you want this handled automatically, you could add `62628c0` to a `.git-blame-ignore-revs` file.
- **Troy Goode appears under two emails.** I counted `<email redacted>` and `troy@laptop.example` as the same person; the repo has no `.mailmap` saying so. Counted separately, `<email redacted>` alone still wins JS with 473.

In the other file types, the second-place owners are:
- **`.md`:** Jon Church (72) and Douglas Christopher Wilson (67).
- **`.yml`:** Íñigo Marquínez Prado (94).
- **`.json`:** Troy Goode (12).

=== ANSWER B ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Skill: {"skill": "tool:skill"}
 - Bash: node /private/tmp/r13/final/ours-src/bin/<tool> --group-by ext --merge-identities
 - Bash: node /private/tmp/r13/final/ours-src/bin/<tool> --group-by ext 2>&1 | sed -n '1,12p;/^\.md/,/^\.md/p' | head -20; git show --stat --format='%h %an %s' 62628c0 | tail -6
FINAL ANSWER:
Each figure below has two line counts: non-blank lines first, then lines counting blank lines. All numbers come from `surviving-lines --group-by ext --merge-identities`, run at HEAD (62628c0) across all 18 files, which is 1,662 non-blank lines (1,995 counting blank lines).

| Type | Size | Top owner | Next |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` (7 files) | 1,032 / 1,202 | **Troy Goode**: 564 (54.7%) / 676 (56.2%) | Douglas Christopher Wilson: 362 (35.1%) / 394 (32.8%) |
| `.yml` (6 files) | 314 / 375 | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 101 (32.2%) / 127 (33.9%) | Íñigo Marquínez Prado: 80 (25.5%) / 95 (25.3%) |
| `.md` (2 files) | 253 / 341 | **Troy Goode**: 71 (28.1%) / 116 (34.0%) | Jon Church: 59 (23.3%) / 72 (21.1%) |
| `.json` (1 file) | 40 (no blank lines) | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 20 (50.0%) | Troy Goode: 12 (30.0%) |
| no extension (2 files, e.g. LICENSE) | 23 / 27 | **Douglas Christopher Wilson**: 21 (91.3%) / 21 (77.8%) | Troy Goode: 2 (8.7%) / 6 (22.2%) |

**Things that affect these numbers:**
- **Troy Goode's two addresses were combined, but that's a guess.** The tool matched <email redacted> and troy@laptop.example by name and address only; nothing was written to `.mailmap`. Counted separately for `.js`, the gmail address has 520 (50.4%) / 628 (52.2%) and the laptop address has 44 (4.3%) / 48 (4.0%). That still makes Troy the top `.js` owner. Every `.md` line of Troy's is from the gmail address, so that row doesn't change.
- **The 62628c0 reformat was set aside.** That commit ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes") rewrote 1,016 lines in 4 `.js` files. I confirmed it with `git show --stat`. Its lines are credited back to whoever wrote them before. Without this correction, the tool reports that Troy (both addresses together) would have 554 lines instead of 649 across the whole repo, and Douglas 376 instead of 551.
- **Lines are credited to the author, not the committer.** For example, the `.yml` lines from Íñigo and Sebastian Beltran were committed by GitHub but count as theirs.
- **Bots are excluded.** dependabot[bot] has 10 lines and 11 commits, none of them counted.
- **One person appears under two names.** alex94puchades (30 `.js` lines) shares an address with Alejandro Caravaca Puchades, and the tool counts them as one person.
- **Seven possibly mechanical commits were kept in the counts.** The tool flagged them but didn't set them aside, for example 739dc11 "docs: reformat license" (17 lines). I didn't re-run without them.

**Concentration risk by type:** the bus factor here is the smallest number of authors who together hold 50% of the lines.
- **`.js`:** 1. Troy alone holds over half, and he's also the only owner who committed in the last 365 days. The tool flags this as a single point of failure.
- **`.yml`:** 2 (Douglas and Íñigo). Neither has committed in the last 365 days, and 58.0% of the lines belong to inactive people, so the tool flags `.yml` as orphaned.
- **`.json`:** 1 (Douglas). Flagged as a single point of failure.
- **No extension:** 1 (Douglas). Flagged as both a single point of failure and orphaned.
- **`.md`:** 2 (Troy and Jon Church).

These counts show whose lines are still there. They don't measure code quality, review work, or who actually understands the code.

=== ANSWER C ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: git ls-files | sed -E 's/.*\///' | awk -F. '{print (NF>1?$NF:"(none)")}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn; git log --format='%h %an <%ae> %s' -n 8; ls .git-blame-ignore-revs 2>/dev/null; git show --stat 62628c0 | tail -3; git show --stat b2005d9 | tail -3
 - Bash: git show b2005d9 | head -40; run(){ for f in $(git ls-files); do b=$(basename $f); case $b in *.*) e=${b##*.};; *) e="(none)";; esac; git blame --line-porcelain "$@" -- $f | sed -n 's/^author //p' | sed "s|^|$e\t|"; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr; }; echo RAW; run; echo IGNORE-FMT; run --ignore-rev 62628c0
 - Bash: for f in $(git ls-files '*.js'); do git blame --line-porcelain --ignore-rev 62628c0 -- $f | awk -v f=$f '/^[0-9a-f]{40} /{c=$1} /^author /{sub(/^author /,"");print f"\t"$0"\t"substr(c,1,7)}'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -15; git show --stat 02afce3 | tail -2
 - Bash: for f in $(git ls-files '*.js'); do git blame -w -M --line-porcelain --ignore-rev 62628c0 -- $f | sed -n 's/^author //p'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -4
FINAL ANSWER:
Here is who owns the most surviving lines (by `git blame` on `HEAD`) for each file type:

| Extension | Files | Top owner | Lines | Runner-up |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` | 7 | **Troy Goode** | 676 | Douglas Christopher Wilson (394) |
| `.md` | 2 | **Troy Goode** | 104 | Jon Church (72) |
| `.yml` | 6 | **Douglas Christopher Wilson** | 117 | Íñigo Marquínez Prado (94) |
| `.json` | 1 | **Douglas Christopher Wilson** | 20 | Troy Goode (12) |
| `.gitignore` | 1 | **Douglas Christopher Wilson** | 4 | Troy Goode (1) |
| no extension (`LICENSE`) | 1 | **Douglas Christopher Wilson** | 17 | Troy Goode (5) |

**The `.js` result depends on one commit.** The latest commit, `62628c0` by Fiona Formatter ("reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes"), rewrote 1,016 JS lines. It only changed formatting. A plain `git blame` credits all 1,016 lines to Fiona, which would make her the `.js` owner. I didn't count that commit, and I also ignored whitespace changes. That way each line goes back to whoever actually wrote it. If you ignore the commit but still count whitespace, Fiona keeps 237 lines and Troy still leads with 520.

The repo has no `.git-blame-ignore-revs` file. If you add one listing `62628c0`, GitHub's blame view and `git blame` (once `blame.ignoreRevsFile` is set) will skip the reformat automatically.

Two smaller points:
- Troy's commit `b2005d9` only adds `// checked` comments to 12 lines in `lib/index.js`. Even without those lines he still leads `.js` by a wide margin.
- I grouped authors by name. Nobody appears under more than one name, so this doesn't change any result.
